    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-112024",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x-24,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 & 2 & -24 \\\\  &  & 2 & -14 & 24 \\\\  & -1 & 7 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+7x-12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-7x+12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)(x-3)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+2)(0-3)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(2)(-3)(-4) \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an  $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,3,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-24,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/e41620d89bb720602c0271428847e05a6655b8ff1e037c0b408972ceaf252cec.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
